1
00:00:00,238 --> 00:00:04,484
Hello everyone and welcome to a talk by the Terraform Plugin SDK team.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:10,404
This is a modern Terraform plugin framework enabling the next generation of Terraform providers.

3
00:00:13,038 --> 00:00:19,268
My name is Katie Mo and I'm a senior software engineer on the Terraform Plugin SDK team at HashiCorp.

4
00:00:22,286 --> 00:00:30,180
I'm going to tell you a bit about the history of Terraform plugin development, how we got here, and how we designed the plugin SDK if we built it today.

5
00:00:31,022 --> 00:00:35,492
I'll then introduce the new experimental framework we've built that follows those design principles.

6
00:00:36,686 --> 00:00:44,480
Then Brian Flad will demo some example provider code and finally Patty Carver will talk about the future of provider development.

7
00:00:46,382 --> 00:00:52,548
The Terraform Plugin SDK is behind almost every Terraform provider in existence today, and it's seven years old.

8
00:00:53,262 --> 00:01:01,220
It started out as a package in the Terraform Go module, and we split it out into a separate Go module shortly after I joined HashiCorp in 2019.

9
00:01:02,766 --> 00:01:06,180
Since then, the Terraform provider ecosystem has grown massively.

10
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:11,716
Terraform itself has changed a lot and added new features and capabilities.

11
00:01:12,366 --> 00:01:19,268
The plugin SDK's abstractions aren't a match for those abstractions anymore, and we're constantly trying to catch up while dealing with legacy code.

12
00:01:20,206 --> 00:01:27,972
If you'd like to know more about the history of provider development, Paddy and I gave a talk at HashiConf Digital 2020 called The History of Terraform Providers.

13
00:01:29,934 --> 00:01:35,364
Sometime last year we started talking about what it would look like if the plugin SDK were written today.

14
00:01:36,142 --> 00:01:41,476
So using that, I'm going to take you through the design goals we think a framework written today would have.

15
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:46,020
Firstly, we want provider code to be unit testable.

16
00:01:46,638 --> 00:01:52,804
When working on a Terraform provider today, our feedback loop most likely involves running acceptance tests.

17
00:01:53,518 --> 00:01:57,700
These tests can be expensive, quite literally, costing time and money to run.

18
00:01:58,766 --> 00:02:04,676
Allowing for unit testable components means quicker feedback loops, more test coverage, and a more solid foundation.

19
00:02:07,118 --> 00:02:09,348
The second principle we're calling adaptability.

20
00:02:10,446 --> 00:02:15,440
We should be able to add features to the SDK and adapt to new capabilities in Terraform.

21
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:16,900
It needs to be forward compatible.

22
00:02:17,870 --> 00:02:24,292
This is difficult in today's SDK since its underlying abstractions no longer match Terraforms and it involves a lot of shims.

23
00:02:26,382 --> 00:02:29,380
An SDK written today would be easy to learn.

24
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:32,900
We want to enable anyone to build a Terraform provider.

25
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:36,644
Learning Terraform provider development shouldn't be a second job.

26
00:02:37,198 --> 00:02:41,760
You should be able to pick up provider development alongside learning Go and alongside your day job.

27
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:42,852
It doesn't need to be hard.

28
00:02:45,198 --> 00:02:47,268
The provider code should be concise.

29
00:02:48,142 --> 00:02:53,540
Less verbosity, less typing, less code, and fewer places for bugs to hide.

30
00:02:56,046 --> 00:02:58,308
Today's SDK would be more Go idiomatic.

31
00:02:58,894 --> 00:03:06,852
As much as possible, a Terraform provider should use standard Go code, so no empty interfaces as function parameters, for example.

32
00:03:09,326 --> 00:03:12,516
The SDK would fail early and loudly with less branching.

33
00:03:13,166 --> 00:03:17,360
With less branching, errors would be easier to catch and fewer tests would be needed.

34
00:03:19,086 --> 00:03:25,860
Finally, we think that if the Terraform plugin SDK were designed today, it would be with automated code generation and other tooling in mind.

35
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:32,164
Provider generation and provider development shouldn't be a huge unassisted human effort.

36
00:03:32,558 --> 00:03:39,620
We're constantly trying to find the interfaces that allow us to specify exactly what is necessary in our provider code and no more.

37
00:03:40,398 --> 00:03:46,724
We're also designing with an eye for where tooling can hook in and how to expose provider semantics to that tooling.

38
00:03:49,454 --> 00:03:55,620
We're pleased to announce the release of the Terraform Plugin Framework, a next generation SDK for Terraform Provider Development.

39
00:03:56,366 --> 00:04:00,548
An experimental version of Terraform Plugin Framework is available right now.

40
00:04:00,974 --> 00:04:06,148
This is an early iteration that we don't have full feature parity with version 2 of the plugin SDK yet.

41
00:04:06,542 --> 00:04:09,412
However, it can be used today to build a Terraform provider.

42
00:04:09,582 --> 00:04:17,540
And please do, because we want as much feedback as possible so we can find out what works and what doesn't, and make all those breaking changes as soon as possible.

43
00:04:19,758 --> 00:04:23,620
I'm going to show you quickly how we've put those design goals into practice so far.

44
00:04:24,846 --> 00:04:30,820
To make the provider code unit testable, we've focused on making the CRUD function arguments and basic types as simple as possible.

45
00:04:32,654 --> 00:04:36,356
So you can mock them in tests and they're easy to construct.

46
00:04:36,768 --> 00:04:39,364
Here are two basic types, config and state.

47
00:04:40,046 --> 00:04:45,956
In the provider's unit tests, you can just make instances of the struct to test how the CRUD functions respond to different inputs.

48
00:04:46,448 --> 00:04:51,076
This means you don't have to call your acceptance tests if you just want to test your create function, for example.

49
00:04:52,718 --> 00:04:55,108
The create function itself might look a bit like this.

50
00:04:55,726 --> 00:05:00,100
So you'll see there are three parameters context, a request, and a response.

51
00:05:01,102 --> 00:05:04,608
These contain the config and state values, and it's clear which is which.

52
00:05:04,942 --> 00:05:10,116
The request contains the prior state, and you'll set the updated state in the response.

53
00:05:12,462 --> 00:05:14,528
The second principle is adaptability.

54
00:05:18,830 --> 00:05:19,168
Excuse me.

55
00:05:19,854 --> 00:05:24,164
Here you'll see one of those request types that is the argument for the create function I showed you before.

56
00:05:25,134 --> 00:05:30,756
In future the framework can add more data here, and so we can make backwards compatible changes to the SDK.

57
00:05:33,294 --> 00:05:35,620
Here's what a resource looks like in the framework code.

58
00:05:35,888 --> 00:05:38,212
It's an interface you fill in the provider code.

59
00:05:40,208 --> 00:05:47,876
So if in the future we wanted to add a new resource interface, say resource with import, we could do that without the provider code needing to change.

60
00:05:50,254 --> 00:05:51,940
Thirdly, we think it's easy to learn.

61
00:05:52,302 --> 00:05:53,860
Here's an example resource schema.

62
00:05:54,350 --> 00:05:56,964
You'll see it's similar to SDKB2, which is fine.

63
00:05:57,390 --> 00:06:02,980
We've taken the opportunity to make the type system even easier to use and also clearer while making it more expressive.

64
00:06:05,102 --> 00:06:06,660
The provider code is concise.

65
00:06:07,054 --> 00:06:09,088
This is all you need to implement delete.

66
00:06:09,408 --> 00:06:12,228
You'll get the attribute value from the request state.

67
00:06:13,518 --> 00:06:21,220
You call your API client delete function, and then you call a function on the response argument to remove the resource from the state.

68
00:06:24,622 --> 00:06:30,276
Now we've made things more go idiomatic and remove the empty interface from CRUD function signatures.

69
00:06:31,182 --> 00:06:40,740
In SDK v2, the empty interface meta parameter was there to give the resource access to the API client we configure on the provider inside the resource CRUD functions.

70
00:06:41,358 --> 00:06:52,356
In Terraform Plugin Framework, you just store that API client on your own provider struct with your own API provider client type, and then you have it available strongly typed inside your resource functions.

71
00:06:55,182 --> 00:06:58,052
Next, the code should be reliable and it should fail fast.

72
00:06:59,054 --> 00:07:01,252
Here's one way to get a value from the config.

73
00:07:02,158 --> 00:07:10,852
You can define your own strongly typed resource data struct, my resource data, and marshal the config into that, kind of like marshalling JSON.

74
00:07:11,566 --> 00:07:15,844
Then you'll handle any error, and afterwards you don't need to make any type assertions.

75
00:07:16,622 --> 00:07:23,968
We're relying so when we write provider code, we can rely on the compiler more now so we don't have to test as much in our provider code.

76
00:07:26,542 --> 00:07:34,528
We're enabling both provider generation and other tooling by building things in that we know tools like the language server and docs generator will need.

77
00:07:37,294 --> 00:07:42,116
Finally, I want to highlight some of the features of the Terraform plugin framework we think are particularly important.

78
00:07:43,182 --> 00:07:48,964
There's better visibility of config state and plan values, which are separately and unambiguously accessible.

79
00:07:49,774 --> 00:08:02,052
There's first class support at last for null and unknown values, so we can tell when a user has omitted a value from config, when they've set it to an empty or zero value, or if the value isn't known yet, for example, if it's computed.

80
00:08:03,342 --> 00:08:11,076
The type system of the new framework is more powerful, so we can use the new nested attributes just added to Terraform and future Terraform type changes.

81
00:08:11,502 --> 00:08:15,812
Finally, there's support for custom types which fill that type interface from the framework.

82
00:08:16,174 --> 00:08:22,820
So for example, you could define a timestamp type in the provider code, or you could import another Go module that provides it.

83
00:08:25,902 --> 00:08:30,020
I've talked about the design goals for the framework and I've shown you how we've aimed to meet them.

84
00:08:30,574 --> 00:08:35,332
Now I'm going to hand over to Brian Fladd, who will build an example provider using this framework.

85
00:08:37,934 --> 00:08:38,880
Thanks so much, Katie.

86
00:08:39,278 --> 00:08:42,980
Let's dive right into what a real world example using this framework would look like.

87
00:08:43,758 --> 00:08:46,308
Let's say you wanted to create a service to manage this conference.

88
00:08:46,894 --> 00:08:53,508
Call it the HashiTalks API, which we then build a Terraform provider on top of for managing this conference's configuration.

89
00:08:54,350 --> 00:08:56,676
What concepts would we want to see in this provider?

90
00:08:57,230 --> 00:09:01,284
How about talks, presentation sessions like the one you're attending right now?

91
00:09:02,062 --> 00:09:06,692
Speakers, the people presenting these talks, like such as myself and some information about us.

92
00:09:07,438 --> 00:09:11,620
And workshops, educational sessions that you might have attended before.

93
00:09:12,878 --> 00:09:21,668
But before we take a look at the actual provider code, let's take a look at an NTREform configuration so we can get a sense of what we're trying to accomplish with this example provider.

94
00:09:24,014 --> 00:09:28,400
Here we have the provider configuration that's taking in an API endpoint.

95
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:31,044
We'll be running an API server here locally.

96
00:09:33,102 --> 00:09:39,332
Speaker information, such as Katie, Patty, and I, you know, some information about us, mostly strings.

97
00:09:41,454 --> 00:09:43,108
A talk such as this one.

98
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:46,276
You know, we're passing in all of our speakers.

99
00:09:47,310 --> 00:09:50,660
And, there's a title and description and duration.

100
00:09:52,400 --> 00:10:00,880
And workshop, which looks pretty similar, but it's also providing some more advanced type information, like this map of objects here, as we'll see.

101
00:10:02,222 --> 00:10:08,804
So once we make sure our API is running here, which it is, what happens if we apply this configuration?

102
00:10:10,382 --> 00:10:13,520
From the practitioner standpoint, there's actually not too much different here.

103
00:10:13,806 --> 00:10:16,996
Terraform plan looks mostly the same as it did before.

104
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:23,716
But really, there is some differences and more capabilities that are being provided under a hood.

105
00:10:24,270 --> 00:10:26,660
So let's start taking a look at what those are.

106
00:10:28,430 --> 00:10:31,520
So here we're actually looking at the provider code itself.

107
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:35,236
This is for the actual creating the provider as an instance.

108
00:10:35,760 --> 00:10:46,052
And the provider has a client in it, and this is the HashiTalks API client that we're going to take the provider configuration and use to configure the API client.

109
00:10:47,470 --> 00:10:51,040
And how we do that is by providing a configuration to Terraform.

110
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:57,680
And with how to get that information into the provider itself, we declare it schema.

111
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:03,588
We want to say there's an API endpoint attribute, it's a string type, it's optional, and a token as well.

112
00:11:03,822 --> 00:11:08,420
And they're optional just because we want to allow environment variable configuration.

113
00:11:10,574 --> 00:11:12,740
Here's where the more interesting parts come in.

114
00:11:13,230 --> 00:11:19,460
You can see we have this new Go data type that has type string and this endpoint and token information.

115
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:30,116
And you'll see this type string here has some information that's more than just the actual string itself, such as these unknown and null fields.

116
00:11:31,438 --> 00:11:44,772
Unknown is an interesting case, and we'll discuss this a little bit later, but null is really easy to describe, you know, it's this the configuration is missing, and this is possible in Terraform 012 and later, which introduced the concept of null.

117
00:11:45,680 --> 00:11:53,732
This wasn't available previously to Terraform provider developers in the previous framework, but now we can surface this information.

118
00:11:55,534 --> 00:12:00,000
So, how is this actually getting applied and how is this API client being created?

119
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:03,140
Usually this happens in the provider configure method.

120
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:09,680
So here we're reading information from the configuration into our custom type.

121
00:12:10,094 --> 00:12:15,840
Now you can see that we're passing in this request that has this configuration.

122
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:23,204
And this configuration has all of the information that we could want provided from the Terraform configuration that we saw earlier.

123
00:12:23,918 --> 00:12:37,188
And what we're doing is we're passing in our custom type so all of this information, such as the endpoint and the token, can actually come from Terraform and be put into native GoTypes that we've declared here.

124
00:12:38,702 --> 00:12:44,624
So once we've checked an error for that and made sure it's happened, we can see now that we can do this unknown checking.

125
00:12:44,704 --> 00:12:58,436
And unknown is a more advanced concept to talk about, but in general, just means that you know there might be a resource that was providing the attribute value that was being passed into the configuration.

126
00:12:58,624 --> 00:13:01,924
And the same example of this, let's actually show that.

127
00:13:03,054 --> 00:13:07,556
So here we have a random pet information that gets created.

128
00:13:08,144 --> 00:13:23,972
We're taking the basically the name that comes out of that, the identifier in this case, and passing that in as our token value, which when Terraform is running, you would think that this resource would get applied, and then the provider configuration would actually happen.

129
00:13:24,430 --> 00:13:27,424
But in reality, that's actually not what happens.

130
00:13:28,558 --> 00:13:29,984
And this has caused problems.

131
00:13:30,304 --> 00:13:36,036
This is a situation that can arise if you're creating multiple levels of infrastructure, for example.

132
00:13:37,024 --> 00:13:59,524
A common case that this can occur in, for example, is Kubernetes, where you create a Kubernetes cluster in a cloud provider, and then all of a sudden, when you go to try to manage your Kubernetes configuration in the same Terraform configuration, it gets a little weird, and Terraform doesn't seem to be doing the right things.

133
00:14:00,144 --> 00:14:05,764
So now this is a situation where Terraform provider developers can actually handle it.

134
00:14:06,638 --> 00:14:16,964
And if we run this configuration here, we now can see that we've caught this error as we defined in our provider here.

135
00:14:17,518 --> 00:14:27,044
and we can provide much more information about what they can possibly try as a practitioner to work around this situation or understand it better, which is pretty neat.

136
00:14:28,046 --> 00:14:39,184
So let's say, for example, that there wasn't actually information provided for the token or the API endpoint.

137
00:14:39,630 --> 00:14:44,624
So in this case, you know, we provided the token as a environment variable.

138
00:14:46,286 --> 00:14:54,144
But we can also see that now Terraform understands that, oh, okay, maybe this information isn't available.

139
00:14:54,384 --> 00:14:56,544
We can see that it wasn't provided config.

140
00:14:56,784 --> 00:15:03,044
We can check that the API endpoint might be an environment variable or the token might be an environment variable.

141
00:15:03,726 --> 00:15:07,824
And okay, if we've exhausted all of our options, now we can return errors.

142
00:15:08,224 --> 00:15:13,024
it's also possible for these diagnostics to return warnings as well.

143
00:15:13,134 --> 00:15:28,324
So if we wanted to provide different situational context about what the provider configuration might be or might be changing in the future, or have different semantics, that information could be provided back to practitioners.

144
00:15:29,678 --> 00:15:40,224
So once we've taken all of this and we've passed it through to our HashiTalks client creation, we now have this available to all the resources and the data sources.

145
00:15:41,134 --> 00:15:54,564
And real briefly, providers also need the ability to return back their list of resources and data sources, which looks mostly similar to as it did before, but now is using these interface methods.

146
00:15:57,134 --> 00:16:00,132
So let's take a look at a resource itself.

147
00:16:00,526 --> 00:16:03,584
And you'll notice you'll start seeing some patterns here.

148
00:16:03,904 --> 00:16:10,628
again, you know, we have this custom resource type that we're providing and a get schema method as well.

149
00:16:11,022 --> 00:16:17,668
And you can see, as from our configuration here, this is mostly string information that's being provided along.

150
00:16:19,566 --> 00:16:34,116
But you know, it's similar to before, where we have values that may not be known until after they're applied, such as an identifier that gets created for a resource, and you know, optional required fields, just as before.

151
00:16:34,670 --> 00:16:42,032
And now we can see where this gets even more powerful with this new framework with the advanced go types.

152
00:16:42,574 --> 00:16:51,044
So in this case, we're taking all this string information and encoding it in a few different ways, uh, and so it's actually accessible in our code natively.

153
00:16:51,662 --> 00:16:58,948
You can see that we have, you know, same strings type here, which might be unknown because it is when it's first being created.

154
00:16:59,792 --> 00:17:03,620
we actually have names set here as like an actual string.

155
00:17:05,102 --> 00:17:14,116
we have these titles and other things as string pointers, which also allows us to see that they're nil in this case in the go sense.

156
00:17:15,552 --> 00:17:24,132
and if we take a look at the create code, you know, this will look very similar to what we saw with the provider, except this time we're taking a look at a plan.

157
00:17:24,752 --> 00:17:28,100
But in reality, it looks exactly the same.

158
00:17:28,512 --> 00:17:44,196
And the nice part is is that as we'll see later on, you can access different levels of information that's being provided to these resource operations, such as you know, configuration, plan, and previous state.

159
00:17:46,798 --> 00:17:51,952
We're taking the plan information and we're creating speakers with it.

160
00:17:52,272 --> 00:17:57,412
we can return errors, and then you know, set the information back in the state.

161
00:17:59,534 --> 00:18:06,884
For read, you know, this is looking very similar, except here we're showing an example of just getting a single attribute from the state.

162
00:18:07,438 --> 00:18:11,748
And you know, we wanted to go fetch the ID that we that was created.

163
00:18:12,752 --> 00:18:22,628
we can use this just like the old framework where we can take that information and go call the API to go read the actual resource from the remote system.

164
00:18:23,214 --> 00:18:35,012
And we can do similar things as before where we can remove a resource if it's no longer found and return errors and do everything that you would expect a Terraform resource to do.

165
00:18:37,326 --> 00:18:45,028
We can also see update here, which I'll breeze through because you know it looks very similar, and delete.

166
00:18:45,582 --> 00:18:46,832
All looks very similar.

167
00:18:46,992 --> 00:18:51,876
Where we're either fetching information from the plan or the state and making an API call.

168
00:18:54,446 --> 00:18:57,060
So let's take a look at a more advanced example.

169
00:18:58,766 --> 00:19:00,580
Here we have the talk resource.

170
00:19:01,102 --> 00:19:04,992
And the talk resource you'll see looks, you know, very similar.

171
00:19:05,072 --> 00:19:09,892
It has a schema, but it has more advanced type information, as we're starting to see.

172
00:19:10,574 --> 00:19:14,628
We have the speaker IDs, for example, that is a list of strings.

173
00:19:15,472 --> 00:19:17,956
And what is this list of strings?

174
00:19:18,352 --> 00:19:26,112
Well, hey, we can go store this as a slice of strings in our Go type when we go fetch this information.

175
00:19:26,382 --> 00:19:27,072
That's wonderful.

176
00:19:27,232 --> 00:19:36,196
That makes it very easy for us in the code itself to actually go reference that information, as we can see here.

177
00:19:39,630 --> 00:19:42,340
How about something not possible in the previous framework?

178
00:19:42,830 --> 00:19:49,220
Let's take a look at this advanced attribute that was created here.

179
00:19:50,766 --> 00:19:53,392
So here we actually have a map of objects.

180
00:19:53,552 --> 00:19:59,172
This is not something that was previously possible to create before with the previous framework.

181
00:20:00,334 --> 00:20:19,780
This is a very advanced example, but what we can see here is that even when we get into more complicated and advanced situations, we can still follow similar patterns for actually creating and receiving the information and storing it back within Terraform.

182
00:20:21,838 --> 00:20:24,164
How about some other types, such as nested attributes?

183
00:20:24,814 --> 00:20:29,732
If we take a look at the workshop configuration from earlier, did you notice there was an equal sign here?

184
00:20:31,086 --> 00:20:34,660
The new framework uses attributes for advanced configurations instead of blocks.

185
00:20:35,790 --> 00:20:41,156
We feel this will offer practitioners a better configuration experience, especially when composing modules.

186
00:20:42,670 --> 00:20:45,344
Under the hood though, there's some technical benefits to this as well.

187
00:20:45,678 --> 00:20:47,904
Behaviors such as sensitivity just work.

188
00:20:48,270 --> 00:20:49,508
So what does this look like?

189
00:20:51,470 --> 00:20:54,884
Here we can see the schema for this, which is much simplified from before.

190
00:20:55,566 --> 00:21:05,764
If we take a look at the data being passed to the functionality for the resource, we'll see that it's also very native and then Go types, just as the other attributes were previously.

191
00:21:07,310 --> 00:21:08,132
It's pretty neat, right?

192
00:21:09,838 --> 00:21:13,636
So before we forget, let's go ahead and destroy our infrastructure.

