00:21:15,022 --> 00:21:20,516
Luckily, we're in development environments, so no need to worry about destroying production.

194
00:21:25,742 --> 00:21:26,304
And there you have it.

195
00:21:26,464 --> 00:21:30,532
We have an end example of a Terraform provider written the new framework.

196
00:21:31,566 --> 00:21:33,860
We're very excited to see what you'll build with this.

197
00:21:34,670 --> 00:21:47,184
What I just showed was only pieces of what is available for the very first release of the framework, but very soon there will be support for provider data source, resource, and schema level validation, as well as resource and schema level plan modifications.

198
00:21:47,758 --> 00:21:50,884
Patty Carver will go much further into the future of the framework.

199
00:21:51,278 --> 00:21:54,064
I'm also sure you're wondering how to get started with all this.

200
00:21:54,350 --> 00:21:56,644
With that, Patty, why don't you tell us some more?

201
00:21:59,662 --> 00:22:00,388
Thanks, Brian.

202
00:22:00,942 --> 00:22:02,624
Hey everyone, my name is Patty Carver.

203
00:22:02,704 --> 00:22:05,956
I'm the engineering lead for the Terraform Plugin SDK team here at HashiCorp.

204
00:22:06,144 --> 00:22:09,828
I use he him pronouns, and you can hack on me on Twitter as at Patty Carver.

205
00:22:10,606 --> 00:22:18,948
So Katie started us off today giving us an overview of this new framework, the design principles that went into it, and some of the background around why we felt it was necessary.

206
00:22:19,566 --> 00:22:26,116
Brian then showed us how we were going to build a provider with it, and so my job here today is to tell you about what's next.

207
00:22:26,574 --> 00:22:31,460
And the first question I'm sure is on everyone's mind when can I use this?

208
00:22:32,206 --> 00:22:34,084
I want to get started, when can I use this?

209
00:22:34,958 --> 00:22:38,116
And the answer is that depends.

210
00:22:38,894 --> 00:22:39,972
That's for you to answer.

211
00:22:40,494 --> 00:22:41,892
What's your risk tolerance like?

212
00:22:42,414 --> 00:22:43,664
When are you ready to use it?

213
00:22:43,758 --> 00:22:45,124
When does it meet your needs?

214
00:22:45,664 --> 00:22:47,904
We built a guide to help you answer that question.

215
00:22:48,014 --> 00:22:51,424
It goes into some of the features that we currently haven't implemented yet.

216
00:22:51,598 --> 00:22:54,692
We talk about some of the requirements that the new framework has.

217
00:22:55,086 --> 00:23:01,796
But fundamentally, this is a decision you need to make that's based in your context and is best for you and your practitioners.

218
00:23:03,662 --> 00:23:05,316
We hope you'll consider the framework though.

219
00:23:07,950 --> 00:23:10,864
We've built some documentation here to help you get started with that.

220
00:23:10,990 --> 00:23:13,984
If you do decide to consider the framework, we're excited about it.

221
00:23:14,158 --> 00:23:15,664
We hope you're excited about it.

222
00:23:15,886 --> 00:23:24,804
I think that there's going to be a lot of really great information in those documents, and we hope it's a really gentle and easy introduction to provider development using the framework.

223
00:23:25,646 --> 00:23:27,904
Next question is what about SDKV2?

224
00:23:28,064 --> 00:23:29,424
Does this mean it's deprecated?

225
00:23:29,504 --> 00:23:30,564
Is it going away?

226
00:23:31,374 --> 00:23:32,864
It's not going anywhere for a while.

227
00:23:33,166 --> 00:23:39,236
SDKV2 is built over a thousand providers and it's relied on by some of the largest companies in the world.

228
00:23:40,014 --> 00:23:42,224
We're not going to pull the rug out from under it.

229
00:23:42,304 --> 00:23:45,892
It's not deprecated, it's not going away anytime soon.

230
00:23:46,222 --> 00:23:52,068
It is still safe to build with that in production, and we will still continue to support it as per our support policies.

231
00:23:54,158 --> 00:23:54,948
What's next?

232
00:23:55,470 --> 00:23:56,516
What's coming next?

233
00:23:56,704 --> 00:23:58,596
Well, let's talk about a roadmap a little bit.

234
00:23:59,086 --> 00:24:00,784
First, we want to close the grap, the gap.

235
00:24:00,864 --> 00:24:04,544
There are some things that SDKv2 can do right now that the framework can't.

236
00:24:04,782 --> 00:24:12,464
There are things the framework can do that SDKv2 can't, as we've shown you today, but there are still a few features that we have not that have not made their way into the framework yet.

237
00:24:12,624 --> 00:24:17,828
We would like to implement those and kind of close that gap so there's no reason left not to use the framework.

238
00:24:18,798 --> 00:24:19,984
We want a smooth migration.

239
00:24:20,144 --> 00:24:23,984
As I mentioned, there are over a thousand providers built on SDKV2.

240
00:24:24,302 --> 00:24:27,236
And right now the migration story for them is a little bit rough.

241
00:24:27,566 --> 00:24:32,932
It's going to involve some breaking changes, it's going to involve breaking changes your practitioners will see.

242
00:24:33,198 --> 00:24:37,584
It's not a story that is ready to be used by everybody yet.

243
00:24:37,744 --> 00:24:40,100
If you're adventurous, I hope you'll give it a shot.

244
00:24:40,334 --> 00:24:46,944
But for everyone else, if you're not building a green field provider, know that there's going to be some rough bumps there, and we want to make those go away.

245
00:24:47,024 --> 00:24:52,868
We want to make that as easy and intuitive as possible for provider developers to increase adoption.

246
00:24:53,326 --> 00:24:55,104
And then finally, we want to polish.

247
00:24:55,184 --> 00:24:59,332
There are some abstractions that are not up to the design goals that we set for ourselves.

248
00:24:59,598 --> 00:25:06,464
There are things that we set out to do that we didn't have time to do just yet, but we wanted to get your feedback on the stuff that we had started.

249
00:25:06,702 --> 00:25:11,748
So now we're going to set about to polishing some of those decisions and some of those missing pieces.

250
00:25:13,870 --> 00:25:16,704
And eventually we would like this to be SDKV3.

251
00:25:17,024 --> 00:25:18,064
That's not happening today.

252
00:25:18,254 --> 00:25:20,420
That's not even probably happening this year.

253
00:25:21,422 --> 00:25:23,812
we need to get a little bit more mileage on it first.

254
00:25:24,174 --> 00:25:25,188
We need your help.

255
00:25:26,446 --> 00:25:27,556
How do you get involved?

256
00:25:28,782 --> 00:25:29,344
How can you help?

257
00:25:29,504 --> 00:25:29,904
How can you?

258
00:25:30,224 --> 00:25:37,316
This is a really exciting time to be a provider developer, and we're defining the next five or ten years of provider development right now.

259
00:25:37,614 --> 00:25:40,932
And that's super exciting, and we want your help with it.

260
00:25:42,062 --> 00:25:45,828
You can join us on Discussion to use the plugin SDK section.

261
00:25:46,766 --> 00:25:47,652
talk to us there.

262
00:25:47,886 --> 00:25:51,140
All of those posts go directly into our Team Slack channel.

263
00:25:51,344 --> 00:25:53,924
I know that sometimes we don't respond for a few days.

264
00:25:54,478 --> 00:25:56,944
We've been a little bit busy, as you can see.

265
00:25:57,184 --> 00:26:08,676
But that is something that we are paying attention to and a place that we're trying to gather our community so that we can send announcements, so that we can tell you about all the neat things that we're doing and get your opinions and get your feedback.

266
00:26:09,678 --> 00:26:10,532
Build providers.

267
00:26:10,944 --> 00:26:13,104
We need people to build providers with this.

268
00:26:13,264 --> 00:26:25,476
We've got all of the evidence in the world that SDKV2 is sufficient for building providers and the subtractions will stand up to real production workloads because, again, the largest companies in the world are relying on it.

269
00:26:25,984 --> 00:26:35,780
We need that same kind of confidence about the framework before we're comfortable calling it SDK V3, before we're comfortable standing behind it as the interface for building providers for the next seven years.

270
00:26:36,590 --> 00:26:38,544
And then finally, join us on GitHub.

271
00:26:38,734 --> 00:26:41,344
Submit issues and PRs to the GitHub repository.

272
00:26:41,454 --> 00:26:45,264
Tell us what was hard, what didn't work, what didn't work how you expected it to.

273
00:26:45,614 --> 00:26:48,004
Tell us your hopes, dreams, give us feature requests.

274
00:26:48,558 --> 00:26:57,476
We can't promise that we're going to implement all of them, we can't promise that we're going to be able to fix everything, but we definitely want to hear about it, and we can't fix it if we don't know about it.

275
00:26:58,606 --> 00:27:00,356
Here's to the next thousand providers.

276
00:27:00,878 --> 00:27:02,704
Thank you so much for joining us today.

277
00:27:02,926 --> 00:27:08,132
Thank you so much for joining us on the journey of the last seven years, and happy terraforming everyone.

